I don't think there's any way to support the statement "most" since it's
not possible to accurately quantify how many people play.

However, replacing it with "many" is not inaccurate; it's a standard
explanation given (unprompted!) on #nethack as to "how to identify the
luckstone at mines' end".  It _is_ a well-known and oft-used technique.

That said, it really highlights the challenges any fork faces when
trying to move onward -- so much of being "good" at Nethack is based 
around knowledge that isn't derived from in-game actions.

In this case, closing the loophole isn't the problem, but deceiving a
player who is using a common tactic is -- it's tantamount to Grunthack's 
"hidden" Izchak code for people who try to kill him, which is a big ol'
case of silliness.

Put another way, expectations from vanilla are set to a specific level,
and without any way for a player to know that something has changed,
it's not a good idea to drastically alter those expectations... or to
alter them without the player being aware that something different is
happening.

